An Alert (the Alert / Message Box page):
An Alert (the Alert / Message Box page) is for what must be answered before the user goes on: it is modal, centred on its window and keeps the keyboard focus until a button is pressed. A toast is for what the user should see but need not answer now: it sits in a corner, never takes the focus, and goes by itself - low 5 s, normal 8 s, critical stays; the pointer resting on it holds it.

From an application, post a system.notification on UltraMessage:
  UltraMessage::SystemNotification n;  n.appId = "org.example.myapp";  n.appName = "MyApp";
  n.summary = "Export finished";  n.actions = {{"default", "Open"}};  n.launch = "file=report.pdf";
  UltraMsg_Post(endpoint, UltraMsgTopics::SystemNotification, UltraMessage::MakeSystemNotification(n));
The desktop's notification service shows it - on ULTRA OS the desktop's toast host, as here; on GNOME, Plasma or Windows their own - and a click comes back as system.notification.action.
Register for the clicks once at the start, so one on a Windows toast reaches the application even after the bus is gone (UltraCanvasNotificationActivation.h):
  UltraCanvas::RegisterNotificationActivator("org.example.myapp", [](const auto& click) { Open(click.launch); });

A desktop shell runs the host itself: UltraCanvasNotificationToastHost toasts; toasts.SetScreenMargins(0, 0, barWidth, 0); toasts.Connect();

